//! core/tasks — running a piece of a program more than once at a time.
//!
//! One function. `parallel(ctx, items, f)` runs `f` over every item and answers
//! the results **in the items' order**, whatever order the work finished in,
//! and hands each call the item's own index.
//!
//! That order promise is the whole reason this module has one signature rather
//! than a family of them. No test can pin a concurrent operation whose answer
//! depends on which task won: this language has no way to say "assert one of
//! these six outputs", so a result list in completion order would be untestable
//! by construction. Fixing the order costs a `[B]` the runtime fills in rather
//! than appends to, and buys a program whose answer is the same on every backend
//! and on every run.
//!
//! `f` receives the caller's whole context as its first parameter, so a task can
//! do anything its caller could — read a file, make a request, sleep — and
//! nothing its caller could not. It arrives as a *parameter* rather than by
//! capture because a lambda may not capture an effect-carrying value (SPEC
//! 10.6).
//!
//! That is `C` in the signature below and `C` again in `effect Tasks`, and it is
//! **not** `Self`. `Self` is whatever implements `Tasks` — `host.tasks`, or a
//! test's double — which grants that one effect and nothing else, so a step
//! handed it could not read a clock or allocate. The one line of this module's
//! body is `ctx.parallel(ctx, items, f)`, and the repetition is the whole point:
//! the first says who does the scheduling, the second says what the work is done
//! with.
//!
//! There is no detached spawn and no handle to join. Every task has finished
//! before `parallel` returns, so a task cannot outlive the context that granted
//! it. That keeps "a program that never names `host.fs` cannot read a file" true
//! of the program's lifetime and not only of its call graph.
//!
//! How much of it actually runs at once is a property of the platform, and
//! deliberately not of the signature:
//!
//! | Backend | Today |
//! |---|---|
//! | JavaScript | genuinely concurrent — it starts the tasks together and awaits them together, so two that wait overlap. Two that *compute* do not: the engine has one thread |
//! | native, `--release` | genuinely **parallel** — each task runs on a carrier of its own, on a processor of its own, so two that wait overlap and two that compute overlap |
//! | native, `buri run` | sequential, in index order, on the calling carrier |
//!
//! All three answer the same list, and that is the whole of what this module
//! promises. The release backend is the one row where `parallel` buys more
//! processors rather than only overlapped waiting, and it earns the name.
//!
//! The development backend is sequential because a program it builds has one
//! Buri stack. A waiting task still holds a frame in it, so a second task could
//! not have one. That is a fact about where its frames live, not a scheduler
//! nobody has written yet, and it changes no signature, no result and no test —
//! which is the point of writing the order promise down first rather than after.
//!
//! `parallel` is `mapCtx` with an index and a scheduler, but it does not belong
//! in `core/list`, because it is not a fact about lists. Its bound carries
//! `Tasks`, which is authority: a signature that names it says the caller was
//! granted the right to run its own work concurrently, and that has to be
//! visible where a reader looks for a program's capabilities.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Stdout, Tasks };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/io" import * as io;
//! from "core/list" import * as list;
//! from "core/str" import * as str;
//! from "core/tasks" import * as tasks;
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Alloc: host.alloc,
//!         Stdout: host.stdout,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     let labelled = tasks.parallel(ctx, ["a", "b", "c"], fn(c, i, name) => {
//!         str.format(c, "${i}:${name}")
//!     });
//!     let _ = io.println(ctx, labelled.join(ctx, " ")).ignore();
//!     .Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! prints `0:a 1:b 2:c` — the items' order, whichever task finished first.

from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Tasks };

/// Runs `f` over every item and answers the results in the items' order.
///
/// The `Int` `f` receives is the item's own index, so a task knows where it is
/// without the closure counting. It could not count anyway: a lambda has nowhere
/// to keep a counter.
///
/// An empty list answers an empty list and runs nothing.
export fn parallel<C: Alloc + Tasks, A, B>(
    ctx: C,
    items: [A],
    f: fn(C, Int, A) => B,
): [B] {
    ctx.parallel(ctx, items, f)
}
